Commands above must come from repository scripts/configuration/CI or a
successful project discovery command. When a category is not configured, record
an explicit documentation gap; omit an inapplicable section only through an
approved focused merge. Do not invent commands or leave a required section blank.

For changed source files, map each applicable mechanical check to the selected
language profile in `docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md`. A prose baseline does not prove
formatter, linter, compiler, or static-analysis compliance, and a tool pass does
not prove architecture, decomposition, or naming judgment that the tool cannot inspect.

## Command provenance

{{COMMAND_PROVENANCE}}

For each command record its source, scope, prerequisites, whether it can mutate
the worktree or external state, and expected outputs. Prefer check/dry-run modes.
A mutating formatter or external command requires authorization in the task.

## Validation matrix

{{VALIDATION_MATRIX}}

Start with the smallest relevant check, then run the affected target/suite and required build. Use temporary output/cache directories when supported; do not pollute the repository.

For cross-module or public-contract changes, include consumer tests, contract or
integration tests, compatibility checks, and migration/rollback validation when
applicable. If no such check exists, report the gap rather than claiming safety.

## Diff checks

```bash
git diff --check
git diff --stat
git diff
git status --short
```

Compare against the starting status. Check for secrets, personal data, generated output, editor state, binaries, fixtures, lockfiles, and unrelated formatting according to project policy.

When review evidence shows the same manual correction at least three times or
across tasks, confirm that `docs/CODING_RULES_LOG.md` contains or is proposed to
contain a scoped candidate. Do not mutate enforcement configuration without approval.

## Result record

Report each command, evidence source, scope, pass/fail/not-run, decisive error,
manual checks, and residual risk. Compare status to the recorded starting state.
Required failures mean the task is incomplete or blocked.
